  <!-- ══ 07 THE KEY AI DESIGN DECISION ══ -->
  <section class="bg-[var(--color-surface)] sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <p class="cs-label mb-4">07. The key AI design decision</p>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-6 max-w-3xl">AI could help build the experience. The admin still needed to shape it.</h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-6">
        One of the hardest parts of designing AI Playbooks was deciding what to delegate to AI and where
        people needed to stay involved. We explored how AI could recommend and assemble a Playbook, but I
        didn't want the interaction to jump straight from an AI-generated proposal to automatic assignment.
      </p>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-10">
        The design included deliberate points where the admin could ask for recommendations, review the
        proposed steps, make changes, and approve the result. That created a more deliberate collaboration
        model: AI helps put the experience together, while the admin remains responsible for deciding what is
        appropriate.
      </p>

      <div class="split mb-10">
        <div class="split-col">
          <p class="split-k">AI helps with</p>
          <ul class="split-list">
            <li>Recommending a Playbook</li>
            <li>Proposing steps</li>
            <li>Helping modify the experience</li>
            <li>Supporting template creation</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="split-col split-col--alt">
          <p class="split-k">Admin remains responsible for</p>
          <ul class="split-list">
            <li>Reviewing the proposal</li>
            <li>Editing or removing steps</li>
            <li>Deciding whether to approve and assign</li>
            <li>Choosing whether to save a reusable template</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="quote max-w-3xl mb-10">
        AI could help create the experience. It shouldn't make the final decision for the admin.
      </p>

      <figure class="evidence max-w-2xl">
        <svg class="evidence-icon" width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        </svg>
        <p class="evidence-t">[ADD: Actual admin review and approval interaction]</p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ 08 AN ILLUSTRATIVE SCENARIO ══ -->
  <section class="sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <p class="cs-label mb-4">08. An illustrative scenario</p>
      <span class="scenario-flag">Illustrative scenario — not a customer case study</span>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-6 max-w-3xl">A new scenario, without an existing template</h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-10">
        Imagine an employee transferring from India to Singapore, and the organization doesn't already have a
        Journey for that situation. An admin could ask the AI agent to help create a suitable Playbook. The
        AI-assisted experience could propose a set of relevant steps. The admin would then review the
        proposal, make changes, and decide whether to approve it or save it as a template for future use. The
        employee would receive the resulting guided experience, without needing to understand how the AI
        assembled it.
      </p>

      <div class="rows">
        {[
          { t: "A new scenario emerges", d: "An India-to-Singapore transfer, with no matching Journey configured." },
          { t: "Admin asks AI for help", d: "Rather than starting a configuration from a blank screen." },
