  <!-- ══ 10 REFLECTION ══ -->
  <section class="sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <div class="max-w-2xl">
        <p class="cs-label mb-4">10. Reflection</p>
        <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-8">What I took away from this work</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-[var(--color-ink-2)] leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Journeys reinforced something I care about in enterprise product design: the experience around a
            task can matter as much as the task itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also reminded me that product direction and UX are closely connected. Some of the important
            design work happened before the final screens — working through where the experience should add
            value, how it should build on existing architecture, and how it should stay coherent across
            workflows.
          </p>
          <p>
            With AI Playbooks, the question became more nuanced. It wasn't only about what AI could generate.
            It was also about how admins could understand, shape, and take responsibility for what it
            proposed. That's the part of AI experience design I find especially interesting: making the
            technology useful without making the person feel removed from the decision.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="quote mt-10">From helping people follow a process to exploring how AI can help create one.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

